# .NET Framework → .NET 8 Migration: BackgroundProcessor

## Critical Breaking Changes

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** |
| `ThreadAbortException` | **Never raised** — dead code |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** |
| Manual `Thread` + `volatile bool` | Use `CancellationToken` + `Task` |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass the token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Replaces ThreadAbortException handling
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit loop cleanly — no ResetAbort needed
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        // Signal cancellation (replaces _running = false + Thread.Abort)
        await _cts.CancelAsync();

        // Wait with a timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (_workerTask != await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)))
        {
            // Log warning — task didn't stop in time.
            // There is NO equivalent to Thread.Abort(). You must
            // ensure GetNextItemAsync/ProcessItemAsync respect the token.
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Pitfalls

**1. You can't force-kill work anymore.**
`Thread.Abort()` is gone with no replacement. If `GetNextItem()` blocks on a synchronous call that ignores the token, it will hang forever. You **must** pass `CancellationToken` into every blocking call (DB queries, HTTP requests, `BlockingCollection.Take`, etc.).

**2. `ThreadAbortException` catch blocks become dead code silently.**
The compiler won't warn you. Your rollback logic simply never executes. Replace with `catch (OperationCanceledException)`.

**3. `Thread.ResetAbort()` throws at runtime, not compile time.**
If you miss it during migration, it crashes in production on the first cancellation path.

**4. Sync-over-async during migration.**
If you can't make `GetNextItem()` async immediately, wrap it to respect cancellation:

```csharp
// Temporary bridge — not ideal, but cancellable
private async Task<Item> GetNextItemAsync(CancellationToken token)
{
    // If the underlying call supports cancellation natively, prefer that.
    // Otherwise, this at least lets the loop exit:
    return await Task.Run(() => GetNextItem(), token);
}
```

⚠️ This only cancels the *wait*, not the work itself. The sync `GetNextItem()` may still run to completion on a thread pool thread.

**5. `volatile bool` is insufficient for cooperative shutdown.**
A `volatile bool` can't wake a thread blocked in `WaitHandle.WaitOne()`, `Console.ReadLine()`, or a socket read. `CancellationToken` integrates with `WaitHandle`, async I/O, and `Register()` callbacks to interrupt blocking waits.
